# Converted to the revised ten-section template (plus section 11); content and tables carried from the previous version.
BOOK = 'Ezekiel'
TESTAMENT = 'Old Testament'
GENRE = 'prophets'
SUBTITLE = 'Glory Departed, Bones Raised, and the Temple Seen'
SECTIONS = [(1, 'Book Setting & Program Identity', [('p', 'This section introduces you to the book by explaining who wrote it, who received it, and what was happening when it was written. It also shows you which of God’s programs the book belongs to — Israel’s prophetic program or the Body of Christ’s mystery program. Knowing the setting and the program identity helps you read the book in its proper context and understand how its teachings fit into the larger story of Scripture.'), ('p', 'Ezekiel begins “among the captives by the river of Chebar,” where the heavens were opened and he saw visions of God (Ezek. 1:1). He is sent to “the children of Israel, to a rebellious nation” (Ezek. 2:3), is made a watchman, sees the glory depart from the house, speaks of the shepherd and the new heart, sees the valley of bones, and is shown a restored temple and land (Ezek. 3:17; 10:18; 34:23; 36:26; 37:1; 40:2; 43:7).'), ('box', 'Program Identity:', 'This book belongs entirely to the Prophecy Program, where the LORD speaks to the house of Israel in exile about judgment, a new heart and spirit, one shepherd, one nation in the land, and the dwelling of the LORD among them (Ezek. 2:3; 36:24–28; 37:22; 43:7).')]),
(2, 'Narrative Flow of Ezekiel', [('p', 'This section gives you a simple walkthrough of how the book unfolds. Instead of diving into details right away, it shows the major movements in the book — how its message begins, how it develops, and how it builds toward its close. Seeing the flow of the book helps you understand the big picture before you study the individual passages.'), ('h3', 'Movement 1 — The Call and the Glory (Chs. 1–3)'), ('p', 'Ezekiel sees the likeness of a throne and is sent to a rebellious nation as a watchman (Ezek. 1:26; 2:3; 3:17).'), ('h3', 'Movement 2 — Judgment on Jerusalem (Chs. 4–24)'), ('p', 'The LORD speaks of the sin of the house, the glory departs from the threshold, and a new heart is promised (Ezek. 10:18; 11:19–20, 23; 18:4, 23, 30).'), ('h3', 'Movement 3 — The Nations (Chs. 25–32)'), ('p', 'Oracles on the nations that rejoiced at Jerusalem’s fall.'), ('h3', 'Movement 4 — Shepherd, New Heart, and Bones (Chs. 33–37)'), ('p', 'The watchman is renewed, the LORD seeks His sheep, gathers Israel, gives a new heart, and raises the valley of bones into one nation under David (Ezek. 33:11; 34:11, 23; 36:24–28; 37:11, 21–27).'), ('h3', 'Movement 5 — The Temple and the Land (Chs. 38–48)'), ('p', 'Gog is spoken of, and the prophet is brought into the land of Israel and shown the house, the glory returning, and the river (Ezek. 38:2; 40:2; 43:2, 7; 47:1, 9).')]),
(3, 'Program Themes in Ezekiel', [('p', 'This section highlights the major ideas emphasized in the book — the big doctrinal themes that shape how Israel should think and live under the covenants. These themes are not verse-by-verse explanations; they are the book’s main emphases, the repeated ideas God wants Israel to carry forward. Seeing these themes together helps you understand the book’s message before you look at the individual truths in the tables.'), ('ol', ['<b>A Rebellious Nation</b> — “I send thee to the children of Israel, to a rebellious nation that hath rebelled against me” (Ezek. 2:3).', '<b>The Watchman</b> — “I have made thee a watchman unto the house of Israel” (Ezek. 3:17).', '<b>The Glory Departs</b> — “The glory of the LORD departed from off the threshold of the house” (Ezek. 10:18).', '<b>A New Heart</b> — “A new heart also will I give you, and a new spirit will I put within you” (Ezek. 36:26).', '<b>The Gathering</b> — “I will take you from among the heathen, and gather you out of all countries, and will bring you into your own land” (Ezek. 36:24).', '<b>One Nation, One King</b> — “I will make them one nation in the land upon the mountains of Israel” (Ezek. 37:22).', '<b>The Covenant of Peace</b> — “I will make a covenant of peace with them; it shall be an everlasting covenant with them” (Ezek. 37:26).'])]),
(4, 'Christ in Ezekiel', [('p', 'This section gathers what the book teaches about Christ — through foreshadowing, typology, and prophetic anticipation. These statements help you see the book’s Christ-centered trajectory before you study the doctrinal truths in detail. By collecting these points together, you get a clear picture of how Ezekiel points to Christ.'), ('ol', ['<b>The One Shepherd</b> — “I will set up one shepherd over them, and he shall feed them, even my servant David” (Ezek. 34:23) is reported as written.', '<b>The Spirit Within</b> — “I will put my spirit within you” (Ezek. 36:27) belongs to the new covenant promised to Israel.', '<b>The Valley of Bones</b> — “These bones are the whole house of Israel” (Ezek. 37:11) is the LORD’s own explanation of the vision.'])]),
(5, 'Israel, the Law, and the Covenants in Ezekiel', [('p', 'This section helps you see how Ezekiel speaks about Israel, the law, and the covenants without mixing programs. Ezekiel operates within Israel’s prophetic framework. By gathering these points together, this section shows you how to read Ezekiel in its own setting — as an Israel-program book — while recognizing what it says (and does not say) about the Body of Christ.'), ('p', 'Ezekiel reinforces Israel’s:'), ('ul', ['the house of Israel as the exiles addressed (Ezek. 2:3; 3:17)', 'the gathering into their own land (Ezek. 36:24)', 'a new heart and spirit for Israel (Ezek. 36:26)', 'one nation under David in the land (Ezek. 37:22, 24)', 'the LORD dwelling among the children of Israel (Ezek. 43:7)']), ('p', 'All content belongs exclusively to the Prophecy Program.')]),
(6, 'Israel-Program Distinctions', [('p', 'This section highlights the teachings in Ezekiel that belong uniquely to Israel’s prophetic program — truths rooted in prophecy, covenant promises, national identity, and God’s dealings with Israel. By gathering these together, this section helps you see how Ezekiel contributes to Israel’s story without borrowing from the Body-of-Christ program.'), ('ul', ['The Rebellious Nation — “I send thee to the children of Israel.” (Ezek. 2:3).', 'The Watchman — “I have made thee a watchman unto the house of Israel.” (Ezek. 3:17).', 'One Heart — “I will give them one heart, and I will put a new spirit within you.” (Ezek. 11:19).', 'The One Shepherd — “I will set up one shepherd over them.” (Ezek. 34:23).', 'The Gathering — “I will take you from among the heathen, and gather you out of all countries, and will bring you into your own land.” (Ezek. 36:24).', 'A New Heart — “A new heart also will I give you, and a new spirit will I put within you.” (Ezek. 36:26).', 'The House of Israel — “these bones are the whole house of Israel.” (Ezek. 37:11).', 'One Nation — “I will make them one nation in the land upon the mountains of Israel.” (Ezek. 37:22).', 'David as King — “David my servant shall be king over them.” (Ezek. 37:24).', 'A Covenant of Peace — “I will make a covenant of peace with them.” (Ezek. 37:26).', 'The Place of His Throne — “the place of my throne, and the place of the soles of my feet, where I will dwell in the midst of the children of Israel for ever.” (Ezek. 43:7).'])]),
(7, 'Truths Table: What Ezekiel Says', [('p', 'This table gathers the key statements, instructions, and teachings in Ezekiel and shows which ones belong to Israel’s prophetic program. Each row lists a truth from the book, explains what it says, and identifies its program identity based on Israel’s covenant setting. This helps you see, at a glance, what Ezekiel teaches directly to Israel and keeps those truths separate from universal principles that apply more broadly.'), ('table', ['Category', 'Truth Revealed in Ezekiel', 'Program Identity', 'Scripture'], [['The Rebellious Nation', '“I send thee to the children of Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezek. 2:3'], ['The Watchman', '“I have made thee a watchman unto the house of Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezek. 3:17'], ['One Heart', '“I will give them one heart, and I will put a new spirit within you.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezek. 11:19'], ['The One Shepherd', '“I will set up one shepherd over them.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezek. 34:23'], ['The Gathering', '“I will take you from among the heathen, and gather you out of all countries, and will bring you into your own land.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezek. 36:24'], ['A New Heart', '“A new heart also will I give you, and a new spirit will I put within you.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezek. 36:26'], ['The House of Israel', '“these bones are the whole house of Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezek. 37:11'], ['One Nation', '“I will make them one nation in the land upon the mountains of Israel.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezek. 37:22'], ['David as King', '“David my servant shall be king over them.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezek. 37:24'], ['A Covenant of Peace', '“I will make a covenant of peace with them.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezek. 37:26'], ['The Place of His Throne', '“the place of my throne, and the place of the soles of my feet, where I will dwell in the midst of the children of Israel for ever.”', 'Israel / Kingdom / Prophecy Explicit Truth', 'Ezek. 43:7']], [20, 34, 26, 20])]),
(8, 'Universal Truths Table (All People, All Time)', [('p', 'This table gathers the truths in Ezekiel that apply to everyone, not just Israel. These are principles rooted in God’s character — things that hold true across all programs and all ages. By separating these universal truths from Israel’s covenant-specific instructions, this section helps you see what Ezekiel teaches all believers everywhere and what it teaches Israel in its own setting.'), ('table', ['Universal Truth', 'Description', 'Scripture'], [['All Souls Are His', '“all souls are mine; as the soul of the father, so also the soul of the son is mine.”', 'Ezek. 18:4'], ['The Soul That Sinneth', '“the soul that sinneth, it shall die.”', 'Ezek. 18:4'], ['No Pleasure in Death', '“Have I any pleasure at all that the wicked should die?”', 'Ezek. 18:23'], ['Turn and Live', '“I have no pleasure in the death of the wicked; but that the wicked turn from his way and live.”', 'Ezek. 33:11']], [28, 48, 24])]),
(9, 'Program-Safe Application Grid', [('p', 'This section shows how to apply the teachings of Ezekiel without mixing programs. The applications come from Israel’s covenant story and from universal truths that apply to everyone. By keeping the applications tied to what the book actually says — and not borrowing commands from Israel’s covenants for the Body of Christ — this grid helps you respond to Ezekiel in a way that is faithful to its setting, clear, and safe.'), ('ol', ['<b>Know all souls are His</b> — The LORD holds every life (Ezek. 18:4).', '<b>Take sin seriously</b> — The soul that sinneth shall die (Ezek. 18:4).', '<b>Trust His mercy toward the wicked</b> — He takes no pleasure in death (Ezek. 18:23).', '<b>Turn from wrong</b> — Turn from the way and live (Ezek. 33:11).'])]),
(10, 'Teaching Outline for Ezekiel', [('p', 'This outline gives you a teacher-ready structure for presenting the book. It follows the narrative movements and highlights the major themes, making it easy to teach Ezekiel in classes, small groups, or personal study.'), ('olr', ['The Call and the Glory (Chs. 1–3) — The watchman sent. <i>(Ezek. 1:26; 2:3; 3:17)</i>', 'Judgment on Jerusalem (Chs. 4–24) — The glory departs. <i>(Ezek. 10:18; 11:19; 18:4, 23)</i>', 'The Nations (Chs. 25–32) — Oracles on the nations.', 'Shepherd, New Heart, and Bones (Chs. 33–37) — The gathering and the nation. <i>(Ezek. 33:11; 34:23; 36:24–28; 37:11, 22)</i>', 'The Temple and the Land (Chs. 38–48) — The house and the glory. <i>(Ezek. 40:2; 43:7; 47:1, 9)</i>'])]),
(11, 'Companion Usage and Study Suggestions', [('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Personal Study'), ('p', 'This Companion is designed to walk alongside you as you read Ezekiel, helping you see both the prophetic structure of the book and the timeless truths rooted in God’s character. Use it slowly, chapter by chapter, letting the narrative movements, themes, and universal truths deepen your understanding. The Program-Safe Applications will guide your heart toward reverence, repentance, and trust in the LORD’s word without confusing Israel’s covenant commands with the Body of Christ’s calling today.'), ('h3', 'How to Use This Companion in Small Groups or Classes'), ('p', 'This Companion provides a clear, structured framework that makes teaching Ezekiel simple and accessible. Each section gives you ready-to-use material: narrative movements for overview, prophecy-program themes for doctrinal clarity, universal truths for discussion, and a pastoral teaching outline for weekly lessons. Use the tables to help your group distinguish Israel’s covenant identity from universal principles, and use the Program-Safe Application Grid to foster meaningful, Christ-centered conversation.'), ('h3', 'How This Companion Fits Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series'), ('p', 'This Companion is part of a larger series designed to help believers read every book of Scripture without blurring God’s prophetic program for Israel and His mystery program for the Body of Christ. Within the Acts Overlap Theology Series, Ezekiel stands firmly in Israel’s covenant story, yet it still reveals universal truths that shape Christian character today. This Companion shows you how to honor the book’s prophetic context while drawing out the timeless principles that apply to all believers in every age.')])]
